#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097425} :activity/id #uuid "0693eaa1-2cbe-58a3-aa5d-c539aa74793e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097425 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097818 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have the two functions $u(x)=2x^3$ and $v(x)=x^2.$ How do we calculate the derivative of their product $(u(x) \\cdot v(x))'?$\n\nTo do that, we can use the **product rule**, which states that the derivative of the product of two functions is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\left(u(x) \\cdot  v(x)\\right)' = u'(x) \\cdot v(x) + u(x) \\cdot v'(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the derivatives of the two functions are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(x) & =(2{x}^{3})^{′}=6{x}^{2}, \\\\ {v}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2})^{′}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we plug all of this into the product rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(x)\\cdot v(x))}^{′} & =(6{x}^{2})({x}^{2})+(2{x}^{3})(2x) \\\\  & =6{x}^{4}+4{x}^{4} \\\\  & =10{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis example was quite simple, and we could have solved it by calculating the product first and then taking the derivative. We can do it now to double check our result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(x)\\cdot v(x))}^{′} & =(2{x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{2})^{′} \\\\  & =(2{x}^{5})^{′} \\\\  & =10{x}^{4}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSometimes, however, we cannot calculate the product algebraically, or it can be tedious to do it. In those cases, the product rule will turn out to be very useful!" :tutorial/id #uuid "958f67f2-8e00-5818-8af7-9f88561ef84c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2622 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ef3171cb-fb23-5d4d-8819-73da9533de18" :step/math-academy-id 12327 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097426}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097426 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096617 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096417 :question/id #uuid "d7a77a89-0535-54da-a246-36ee5b4e9e7f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3634" :question/problem "Differentiate $x\\cos x.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=\\cos x .$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t (x\\cos x)' &=   u'(x) \\cdot v(x) + u(x) \\cdot v'(x)\\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t&= (x)'\\cdot \\cos x + x\\cdot (\\cos x)'\\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t   & = 1 \\cdot \\cos x + x (-\\sin x)\\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t  & =  \\cos x - x \\sin x\t\t\t\t\t\n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b194bf2f-feaa-5656-a3ee-0ff466a39e77" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050420}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530651} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571442}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing a Trigonometric Function"} :step/id #uuid "e27ef05d-bc90-5cce-9885-8f21279044e8" :step/math-academy-id 12328 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097427}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097427 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096618 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096418 :question/id #uuid "6bb0eded-7277-5ab5-8c13-bfc480403b1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3635" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=x^2\\ln x,$ find $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x) = x^2$ and $v(x)=\\ln x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({x}^{2})}^{′}ln⁡x+{x}^{2}{(ln⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =2x\\cdot ln⁡x+{x}^{2}(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =2xln⁡x+x \\\\  & =x(2ln⁡x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f15a60dd-f6a7-577d-bdbc-148032de94c8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186586552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186586560}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing an Exponential or Logarithmic Function"} :step/id #uuid "6cbd3b19-1c1f-519e-b232-315484ae9234" :step/math-academy-id 12329 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097428}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097428 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097819 :tutorial/content "The product rule can also be written using *Leibniz notation*:\n\nIf $y = u(x) \\cdot v(x),$ then \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} \\cdot  v + u \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3c6d5393-f91c-57e5-999c-92eedf550f2e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2623 :tutorial/title "Leibniz Notation"} :step/id #uuid "4a3a2bef-3f64-510c-8bf8-25d81169d680" :step/math-academy-id 12330 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097429}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097429 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096619 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096419 :question/id #uuid "072ce609-b04d-57b0-9c7d-cbb8765c38fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-3636" :question/problem "If a curve $\\mathcal C$ is given by $y=e^x\\sin x,$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\dfrac \\pi 2.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x) = e^x$ and $v(x)=\\sin{x}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = e^x,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos x.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot cos⁡x+sin⁡x\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(cos⁡x+sin⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\dfrac\\pi 2$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\pi /2} & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(cos⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+sin⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(0+1) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi /2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $e^{\\pi/2}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee64135e-9b9f-5429-a4df-efa522042b83" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050711}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536099} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600798}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Product  at a Given Point"} :step/id #uuid "138782c6-ad9e-5b08-b3dd-375476961e1e" :step/math-academy-id 12331}] :activity/title "The Product Rule for Differentiation" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096889}]}
